<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bread and Roses</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">poem published December <a href="/years/1911">1911</a>; first set to music <a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Bread and Roses&quot; began as a slogan of the American women's suffrage movement. Its first known appearance is in an article by the suffrage activist Helen Todd in The American Magazine of September 1911. In June 1910 Todd, a state factory inspector, had joined an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/automobile">automobile</a> campaign across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> for women's right to vote, speaking for working women on wages, conditions and hours.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Helen Todd wrote that the phrase came from a young hired girl in whose family's house she stayed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> campaign, who said she had liked best the talk of women voting so that everybody would have bread and flowers too. In her speeches Todd explained it: life's Bread was home, shelter and security, and the Roses of life were music, education, nature and books. During the Chicago garment workers' strike of 1910 a sign was later reported to read We want bread and roses, too, and in the successful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> suffrage campaign of 1911 banners carried the slogan Bread for all, and Roses, too.</p><p>The source of Todd's inspiration is unknown, but the sentiment closely parallels a saying attributed to the Roman physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, that a man with two loaves should sell one and buy flowers, as bread is food for the body and flowers food for the mind. Mary MacArthur of the British Women's Trade Union League quoted it on a visit to Chicago in April 1907.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>James Oppenheim's poem took Todd's phrase as its title and appeared in The American Magazine in December 1911, credited to a slogan of the women in the West. Its four stanzas imagine women marching and singing, and declare that hearts starve as well as bodies and that the rising of the women means the rising of the race. When Upton Sinclair printed it in his anthology The Cry for Justice in 1915, he tied it to girls carrying a banner in a parade of strikers at Lawrence, Massachusetts. A number of historians consider that account ahistorical.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In June 1912 Rose Schneiderman of the Women's Trade Union League of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> told an audience in Cleveland that the worker must have bread, but she must have roses, too, and her repeated use of the words tied her name to them. Caroline Kohlsaat set the poem to music in 1917; by the 1930s women sang it on picket lines, and since 1932 graduating seniors at Mount Holyoke College have sung it at the Laurel Parade.</p><p>Mimi Fariña set the poem again in 1974, a version recorded by Judy Collins, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> and Ani DiFranco among others. The logo of the Democratic Socialists of America, formed in 1982, was inspired by the slogan, and the film Pride of 2014 shows a Welsh mining community singing the song during the miners' strike of 1984–1985.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bread and Roses” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
